window.GAKS_SEARCH = [{"t":"The SUT model","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model.html","s":"draft","x":"The SUT model Definition One JSON document that declares a system under test: its states (places a journey can be, each with a marker that proves arrival), its elements (named locators), its actions (steps the SUT offers — the edges of the graph), its journeys (named paths through the actions) and its endpoints (the API surface). The contract is SPEC-taf-v1-001-04. Why it exists A test suite is usually a pile of files that encode knowledge of the SUT invisibly. Here the knowledge is written down once, as data: the same model generates the Python suite and the Java suite, refuses broken input with named reasons, and later is the graph that model-based walks run on. Change the model, regenerate, and the suite's diff is the change — visible in a pull request. In this repo sut.model.json per SUT (none yet — lane A-1 builds the loader). Read SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 for the clauses and the refusals."},{"t":"The seeded walk","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk.html","s":"draft","x":"The seeded walk Definition A random test is only useful if it can fail twice. A seeded walk drives a pseudo-random number generator with a recorded seed, so the \"random\" journey it derived is exactly reproducible: same model, same start, same seed — the same steps. The walk's output is a journey declaration in the model's own JSON shape, so the replay is data you can commit, review, and emit — not a log file only a runner understands. python -m taf walk model.json --from board --seed 42 --steps 6 walk.json python -m taf emit --lang python model.json -o suite --journeys walk.json Why it exists MBT without replay is a bug report that says \"it failed once.\" Seed replay turns exploration into a regression asset: commit the seed (or the derived journey), and the failure is a named test forever. --cover prefers untaken edges — the first step toward coverage-directed generation. In this repo taf/graph.py — edges derives adjacency from declared from/to; walk traverses it under random.Random(seed); withjourneys merges derived journeys into a copy of the model. T-taf-006 proves determinism, coverage, and the merge path."},{"t":"The dwell","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell.html","s":"draft","x":"The dwell Definition A {\"dwell\": } step — a declared pause, emitted as a literal page.waitfortimeout(800) / page.waitForTimeout(800) at exactly the step where a watching human needs the screen to hold still. Why it exists Automation that nobody can watch cannot teach. The old framework's visibilitySleep answered two needs with one sleep: letting the page settle and letting the eye follow. tafbase separates them — correctness belongs to markers and CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting; observation belongs to dwell. Keeping them apart is the whole point: a sleep that papers over a timing bug is a lie the suite tells later. In this project dwell is a validated step form (taf/model.py, bad-dwell refusal); both emitters render it; the tracker model pauses 800 ms inside open-tot so a watcher sees the tot island land. Suite-wide pacing is the environment's job — TAFSLOWMO in CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q watch the pause Neighbors wait-for in CONCEPT-dsl-v1-004-01-the-wait; narration in CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label."},{"t":"The wait","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-004-01-the-wait","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-004-01-the-wait.html","s":"draft","x":"The wait Definition Step forms that say how long to look. Implemented today: {\"wait-for\": {\"element\": x}} or {\"state\": y} — an auto-waiting assertion that must hold. Planned (lane H-8): {\"check\": ...}, the soft twin that collects a failure and continues, and {\"wait-any\": [...]} for UIs with alternating states. Why it exists Most flakiness is timing that was never declared. A junior reaches for sleep; a named wait says the intent — hold hard until the marker appears versus log and continue. The old framework kept the whole vocabulary: waitUntilVisibilityOfElement, waitInvisibilityOfElement, waitUntilVisibilityOfEitherElements, with soft variants that logged, saved DOM and screenshot, and went on. Hard fails the step; soft records it. A suite needs both — \"the SUT must be here\" and \"the SUT might offer this\". In this project wait-for is a step form in taf/model.py and both emitters (state → expectarrived, element → tobevisible). check and wait-any are lane H-8, drafted in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01. Touch it Add {\"wait-for\": {\"state\": \"tot\"}} to a do list and run python -m taf check — the loader validates it like any step. Neighbors CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting (the mechanism), CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell (observation, not correctness)."},{"t":"The versioned locator","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator.html","s":"draft","x":"The versioned locator Definition One element, several declarations, keyed by the SUT's version (and, where needed, language): the locator that fits is chosen by nearest declared version at or below the target, with a plain fallback. Why it exists Suites rot fastest at locators, and an app's UI differs between releases. The old framework's VersionedLocator was a version x language matrix — addLocator(515, SPANISH, \"xpath\", ...) — resolved by getBestGuessLocator: largest version <= requested, English fallback. It decoupled the suite from app-version churn; a suite could run QA build 480 and UAT build 515 from one declaration. In this project Planned — lane I-9. Today's elements map one name to one rung of CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder; the versioned form adds variants (v515+, es) that the emitter resolves at codegen — the lookup table becomes generated data, or per-version generated suites, not a runtime map. Touch it Not yet runnable — see lane I-9 in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-011-01-the-locale (the language dimension); CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target (which version a run aims at)."},{"t":"The label","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label.html","s":"draft","x":"The label Definition A human name on an element, action, or step that emitted code narrates as it runs — the old framework's canonicalName + parentName that made every action announce itself: Clicking 'Sign In button' on 'Login Pom'. Why it exists A run's output should read as a story, not as selectors. The label is what a watcher hears; it is what a fault report can quote; and with a timestamp it is the raw material of CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track. The old framework pushed the same narration into document.title — progress visible even inside remote-session videos, a trick worth keeping because it is free. In this project Planned — lane H-8. Today an action narrates only by its model name (open-tot); label makes it prose. Emitted code prints the label before each step — cheap in generated code, invisible when absent. Touch it Not yet runnable — see lane H-8 in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01. Neighbors CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track, CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel."},{"t":"The step language","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-007-01-the-step-language","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-007-01-the-step-language.html","s":"draft","x":"The step language Definition The closed verb vocabulary inside do: and steps: — act (click, fill, check, select), move (navigate), observe (wait-for, expect), call the API (api, save), pace (dwell). A step is one key with its argument; anything else is refused, named. Why it exists A small closed vocabulary is a grammar a person learns in one sitting — and it is not new. The old framework's TSV interpreter ran the same shape as columns: step, action, field name, label, value, screenshot control, locale overrides, ticket. It was field-proven on juniors; generating code from the same grammar is its compile-time heir — what was a runtime interpreter becomes emitted statements a reader can step through. Planned siblings from the same harvest: maybe (the ifPresent idiom — click only if present, never fail), check/wait-any (see CONCEPT-dsl-v1-004-01-the-wait), click-image (see CONCEPT-dsl-v1-008-01-the-image-step). In this project STEPFORMS in taf/model.py is the whole grammar; SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 TAF-3 is the contract; each form renders in both emitters — parity is asserted by tests/testparity.py. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m taf check suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model, CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell."},{"t":"The image step","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-008-01-the-image-step","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-008-01-the-image-step.html","s":"draft","x":"The image step Definition A step that acts on pixels, not the DOM: find a template image on the screen and click it — the old framework's OpenCV matchTemplate used to clear CloudFlare's \"verify you are human\" checkbox, and its Sikuli layer did wait/highlight/click-by-image and OCR. Why it exists Some surfaces have no accessible DOM — anti-bot gates, canvas apps, native dialogs. The image step is the last rung past the last rung: fragile by nature, and honest only when it says so. The better fix is always cooperation — the old framework also sent a tradu-test header so the backend whitelisted automation. Keep both: negotiate when you can, match pixels when you cannot. In this project Deferred — niche. The locator ladder's css/xpath rung is fragile the same way; an image step would carry fragile in the manifest like they do. See RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01, harvest item 19. Touch it Not implemented. The honest placeholder: treat it as a fragile locator when it arrives. Neighbors CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder, CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file (image comparison done right)."},{"t":"The test id","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-009-01-the-test-id","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-009-01-the-test-id.html","s":"draft","x":"The test id Definition A short, stable identifier a test carries — the old framework's ASM011, extracted from the scenario name to drive golden filenames, result folders, and report groups. Why it exists Names are prose; ids are handles. Every artifact a run leaves — ASM011run42desktopstep3Expected.PNG, a rerun manifest entry, a fault report's subject — joins on the id. Parsing it out of a name was the workaround; declaring it (test ASM011 \"...\") makes it a first-class token the model owns and the manifest exports. In this project Planned — today the journey name is the de facto id (taf.manifest.json keys on it). Declared test ids arrive with the artifact grammar of lane J-10 and the rerun manifest of lane L-12. Touch it Read generated/python/ikc3-tracker/taf.manifest.json — the test fields are the ids a declared token will replace. Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file, CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts, CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun."},{"t":"The fixture block","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-010-01-the-fixture","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-010-01-the-fixture.html","s":"draft","x":"The fixture block Definition Arrange through the API, act through the UI: a fixture: block of backend calls a journey runs before its browser steps — the old framework's SOAP/XML command templates that prepared a trader account before the UI test ever opened a page. Why it exists Driving setup through the UI is slow and brittle — the journey then tests the login screen when it meant to test the ticket board. An API fixture is fast, precise, and honest about what is under test. It is also where test data comes from: create the account, seed the ticket, then open the browser. In this project The mechanism half exists — api steps already call endpoints and save response values. The declared fixture: arrangement (runs once before the act, clearly separated from steps under test) is planned; see RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01, item 24. Touch it json {\"api\": \"list-tickets\"} // already runs mid-journey; a fixture block is the planned home Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert — the fixture is the arrange; CONCEPT-dsl-v1-007-01-the-step-language."},{"t":"The locale","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-011-01-the-locale","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-011-01-the-locale.html","s":"draft","x":"The locale Definition A language dimension on the model's data and locators — one data table per language (table[lang][key]), locator variants per locale, and the occasional behavioral flip (the old framework's Arabic runs reversed slider direction). Why it exists A localized SUT is not one SUT — \"Log in\" and \"Ingresar\" are different locators for the same element. The old framework ran 18 languages off xlsx columns and transLabel/transValue row overrides; without the dimension declared, localization testing means a second, drifting copy of the model. In this project Deferred — pairs naturally with CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator, whose matrix is (version x language). It lands when a second-language SUT model needs it; until then the model's elements/data are single-locale. Touch it Not implemented — see RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01, items 5 and 25. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator, CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target."},{"t":"The target","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target.html","s":"draft","x":"The target Definition device: and env: header clauses — Pixel9ProXLchrome, qa, uat — resolved at emit time into the capabilities block and base URL the suite launches with. One token, everything derived. Why it exists The old framework parsed a resolution string at runtime — w10chrome, GalaxyS25Ultrasamsung, iPhone16Prosafari — through 300 lines of factory dispatching on OS, browser, device, local CDP emulation or BrowserStack. It was already a mini-DSL with a runtime interpreter. Generated code does better: the vocabulary is validated at emit time, the emitted suite carries the exact capabilities object, and the tag-to-environment mapping (@qaenv - suite + URL + locator version) bakes in the same way. The layered property files it replaced — root defaults, module config, user overrides — become inputs to codegen, resolved once and printed into the suite header. In this project Planned — lane K-11. Today the only retarget is TAFBASEURL (CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment). Touch it Not yet runnable — see lane K-11 in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-005-01-the-versioned-locator — target version picks locator variants; CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment."},{"t":"The absorption loop","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-013-01-the-absorption-loop","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-013-01-the-absorption-loop.html","s":"draft","x":"The absorption loop Definition The loop that grows the DSL: the language cannot say it → a test is authored by hand in custom/ → the manual pattern recurs or stabilizes → a model clause learns to say it (loader + both emitters + spec + a named refusal) → regenerate, and generated code replaces the manual code. custom/ trends back toward empty. Why it exists A fixed vocabulary eventually fails — the SUT does something the step language never imagined. Two bad answers exist: freeze (the DSL rots) or grow custom/ forever (the DSL becomes a suggestion and the manifest stops describing the suite). The loop is the third: hand-code is scaffolding, the model absorbs it, and the language is the residue of everything that ever needed saying twice. The old framework ran the same metabolism informally — waitUntilVisibilityOfEitherElements and the ifPresent idioms were born as hand-rolled helpers that recurred until they earned a name. Here it is deliberate: wait-any, maybe, soft expect, dwell are the loop already running, fed by the harvest rather than by custom code. In this project custom/ is the seam (planned — the emitter never writes there, the manifest can mark source: hand). A growing custom/ is the DSL reporting which clause is missing — a diagnostic, not a failure. Touch it The newest clauses are the proof: bash grep -E 'wait-any maybe soft dwell' suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-007-01-the-step-language — the vocabulary the loop grows; CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01-the-three-levels — custom/ is where Use reaches back toward Model."},{"t":"The JUnit lifecycle","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-the-junit-lifecycle","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-the-junit-lifecycle.html","s":"draft","x":"The JUnit lifecycle Definition JUnit 5's fixture mechanism: annotated methods JUnit runs around tests. @BeforeAll/@AfterAll run once per class (static, expensive setup like launching a browser); @BeforeEach/@AfterEach bracket every @Test (fresh context, fresh page). It is the same idea as the pytest fixture — setup and guaranteed teardown — expressed as annotations on a base class. java @BeforeAll static void launch() { browser = playwright.chromium().launch(); } @BeforeEach void fresh() { context = browser.newContext(); page = context.newPage(); } @AfterEach void closeContext() { context.close(); } @AfterAll static void shutdown() { browser.close(); } Why it exists Generated Java tests get the same isolation contract as the pytest ones (session browser, per-test context) through a different mechanism — proof that the model's semantics survive the language change and only the idiom moves. In this repo generated-java//src/test/java/taf/generated/BaseTest.java — every generated JUnit suite inherits its lifecycle from this class."},{"t":"The locator ladder","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder.html","s":"draft","x":"The locator ladder Definition The ordered preference for how a test finds a UI element: 1. role + name — what the element is to a user (button \"Save\") 2. label / text / placeholder — what it says 3. testid (data-testid) — a deliberate marker the SUT provides 4. css / xpath — how it happens to be built; last resort, flagged fragile Why it exists Tests that find elements by structure break when structure changes; tests that find them by meaning break only when meaning changes. The ladder is Playwright's own guidance made mechanical: in sut.model.json a locator declares one of the rungs and :bad-locator refuses anything else, so the preference is enforced by the model, not by habit. In this repo SPEC-taf-v1-001-04, elements clause. The emitters translate each rung to the platform's locator API (getbyrole → getByRole). The rung above the rungs — semantic locator kinds The old framework went one step further with kinds like buttonTextLabel and inputTextLabel (\"input labeled 'Email'\" — a label whose for/following-sibling finds the field). Juniors wrote locators without knowing XPath; the emitter compiled the kind into it. Planned for the ladder: named semantic kinds that emit their XPath — see RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01, item 7. Touch it bash grep -A3 '\"marker\"' suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json Neighbors CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting (a locator that auto-waits is a locator that works), CONCEPT-dsl-v1-008-01-the-image-step (the rung past the last rung)."},{"t":"The browser context","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context.html","s":"draft","x":"The browser context Definition Playwright's unit of isolation: a browser.newcontext() is an independent session — own cookies, storage, cache — inside one browser. Each test gets a fresh context, so one test's login or localStorage can never leak into the next. Why it exists The honest alternative is a fresh browser per test, which is slow, or a shared browser, which leaks state and makes failures order-dependent. A context is fast and isolated — test independence by construction, which is what makes generated suites safe to run in any order or in parallel later. In this repo generated//conftest.py — the page fixture creates and closes a context around every test."},{"t":"Auto-waiting assertions","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting.html","s":"draft","x":"Auto-waiting assertions Definition expect(locator).tobevisible() does not check once — it retries until the page catches up or the timeout fails the test. Playwright's locators and assertions wait for the condition instead of racing the browser. Why it exists The classic cause of flaky tests is the race between the test and the app: click() before the button renders, assert before the page settles. Explicit sleep() calls are how suites rot. Auto-waiting makes the honest default also the fast one — the test proceeds the moment the SUT is actually ready. In this repo generated//pages.py — expectarrived() is an auto-waiting marker check; every state transition in a journey waits on the SUT's proof, never on a timer."},{"t":"The page object","i":"CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object.html","s":"draft","x":"The page object Definition A class that is a page or state of the SUT: it holds the page's locators and its meaningful operations, so tests say board.expectarrived() instead of repeating selectors. python class BoardPage: def init(self, page): self.marker = page.locator(\".ikc-dock-theme\") def expectarrived(self): expect(self.marker).tobevisible() Why it exists Locators are the most-fragile line in a suite. When they live in one class per state, a UI change is a one-place fix — and tests read as intent (\"arrive at the board\"), not mechanics. In tafbase the pattern is stricter: a page object's marker is the proof of arrival the model declares, so the class also knows when its page is real. In this repo generated//pages.py — one class per declared state, generated from states in the model (SPEC-taf-v1-001-04)."},{"t":"The pytest fixture","i":"CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture.html","s":"draft","x":"The pytest fixture Definition A function pytest calls to provide something a test needs — a browser, a page, a base URL — and to clean it up after. yield splits the two halves: everything before it is setup, everything after is teardown that runs even when the test fails. python @pytest.fixture def page(browser): pg = browser.newpage() setup yield pg handed to the test pg.close() teardown, always scope= says how often the fixture is made: function (every test — the default), class, module, session (once for the whole run). Why it exists Tests should not repeat their plumbing. Fixtures give each test a fresh, named dependency — and the scope gives control over cost: a browser is expensive (session), a clean page is cheap (function). In this repo generated//conftest.py — the emitted suites get baseurl, browser and page fixtures for free."},{"t":"The virtual environment","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-the-virtual-environment","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-the-virtual-environment.html","s":"draft","x":"The virtual environment Definition A virtual environment — .venv/ in this repo — is a directory that redirects where Python looks for packages. It is not a copy of Python. Inside: .venv/ ├── bin/python a stub — the same interpreter, different eyes ├── bin/pip installs into THIS directory ├── pyvenv.cfg home = /usr/bin — where the real interpreter lives └── lib/python3.12/site-packages/ this project's own package store Running .venv/bin/python is the mechanism: the interpreter reads pyvenv.cfg beside itself, learns include-system-site-packages = false, and resolves imports in .venv/lib/.../site-packages only. \"Activating\" (source .venv/bin/activate) is merely a convenience that puts .venv/bin first on PATH so a bare python resolves there — this repo never relies on it and always names .venv/bin/python explicitly. The path is the activation. Why it exists Packages collide. pip install playwright without a venv writes into one shared site-packages for the whole system: a second project needing another version, or an OS tool written in Python, now fights for the same slots. A venv gives each project its own package store, so pytest 9 here and pytest 7 elsewhere coexist, and so python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install pytest playwright reproduces this toolchain on any machine without touching — or needing permission to touch — the system Python. In this project .venv/ is gitignored — it is recreated, never committed. It holds pytest, playwright, websocket-client, and nothing else; every command in this corpus and in CONTRIBUTING.md calls .venv/bin/python (or .venv/bin/python -m pytest, .venv/bin/python -m taf), never the bare python, so a run cannot silently pick up the system's package set. The generated suites it runs are in generated/; the emitters it runs are taf/. Touch it bash cat .venv/pyvenv.cfg which interpreter it shadows, and how it was made .venv/bin/python -m pip list what this project alone has installed python3 -m venv /tmp/other make a second one — the cost is seconds Delete .venv/ entirely and nothing is lost: python3 -m venv .venv plus the README's install line rebuilds it. Neighbors Environment variables are a different thing sharing the word — CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment. What the venv installs is pytest and Playwright: CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture, CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context."},{"t":"The learning path","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path.html","s":"draft","x":"The learning path Definition The ordered index of this corpus's concept documents, junior-first. The corpus is flat and wiki-linked; this page is the sequence a reader new to test automation can follow, each entry naming its prerequisites. Why it exists ADR-taf-v1-002-01 makes the delivered project a learning surface: every concept the generated code embodies is a page here, and generated files cite their concepts with @concept lines the checker enforces. This page answers \"where do I start?\" The path 1. What a test is → CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert 2. Finding things on a page → CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder 3. Knowing where you are → the state marker (SPEC-taf-v1-001-04, states) 4. The model → CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model 5. From model to suite → the emitter and the manifest (SPEC-taf-v1-001-04) 6. Your own toolchain → CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-the-virtual-environment 7. pytest mechanics → CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture 8. Playwright mechanics → CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context, CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting 9. Organizing a suite → CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object 10. Configuring a run → CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment 11. Closing the loop → CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink 12. Java parity — what \"equivalent suite\" means → CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-the-junit-lifecycle 13. The graph — walks, seeds, coverage → CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk Every entry is a checked citation — the corpus checker refuses a link to a page that does not exist. The domains The path is the on-ramp; the corpus is grouped by what a page teaches: domain teaches --- --- python the language surface — venv, env-adjacent mechanics pytest the test runner — fixtures, hooks, scope playwright the browser library — locators, contexts, waiting java the second emitter's world — JUnit lifecycle, Maven pom the page-object model — organizing a suite testing the discipline — AAA, failure handling, goldens, flakes dsl the model language — steps, locators, targets, walks taf the framework itself — its contract, sinks, artifacts A concept files under the domain it teaches; language-neutral ideas — the TAF contract, the run layout — live under taf, once, never duplicated per language. Three readers, three entries tafbase is three levels — build the framework, model a SUT, use the suite (CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01-the-three-levels). Each reads a different subset: you are read first --- --- a model author dsl — the model, the step language, waits, locators a test user taf + testing — the contract, the sink, failure handling a framework contributor python + pytest + playwright — the machinery A reader never needs all domains — that separation is the DSL First promise itself."},{"t":"The result sink","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink.html","s":"draft","x":"The result sink Definition Where a finished test run sends what it found. A sink is a side channel: the suite never waits on it, and a sink that cannot file warns instead of failing the suite a second time. The first sink is the IKC3 tracker — a red test dispatches :support/file-report over the bus's WebSocket, and the tracker writes a :fault-report frame with its own page. Because the manifest maps each test to its journey, the report names the model element that failed, not only the test. python rid = ikc3sink.filereport( \"ws://127.0.0.1:4856/bus-ws\", \"taf journey failed: boardtotot — TimeoutError …\", url=page.url, dom=page.content()[:50000]) Why it exists A suite that only turns red ends the loop in a log file. The sink closes it: fail → report → ticket → fix → repaired is the quality loop the whole platform demonstrates, and the tracker is both the SUT and the tracker. In this repo taf/sinkikc3.py — the wire protocol and filereport. Emitted verbatim into every Python suite (ikc3sink.py) and driven by the pytestruntestmakereport hook in conftest.py when TAFIKC3WS is set. Verified live in tests/testlive.py (opt-in TAFLIVE=1)."},{"t":"The environment contract","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment.html","s":"draft","x":"The environment contract Definition Every process inherits a set of text key=value pairs from the program that started it — its environment. The shell exports them; the program reads them. This page is the framework's contract over that OS mechanism: the TAF variables every generated suite honors, in every language. Python reads them through os.environ: python os.environ.get(\"TAFBASEURL\", \"http://127.0.0.1:4856\") value, or a default os.environ.get(\"TAFIKC3WS\") value, or None Java reads the same table through System.getenv(). One mechanism, two spellings — one contract, which is why a java page on environment variables does not exist: the variable means the same thing whoever reads it. A variable set for one command applies to that run only: bash TAFSLOWMO=800 .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q (Not this word's other meaning: the virtual environment is a different thing — CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-the-virtual-environment.) Why it exists Generated code is immutable — you edit the model and regenerate, never the output. But where the SUT runs changes run to run. The environment is the seam where run-to-run variation belongs: the same committed suite tests a probe bus, a staging box, or production by setting a variable, not by editing code. The previous-generation framework externalized the same seam into config.properties files; environment variables are the twelve-factor form of the same idea. In this project variable read by meaning ---------------- ----------------------------- --------------------------------------------- TAFBASEURL conftest.py, BaseTest where the SUT lives — probe, staging, prod TAFIKC3WS pytestruntestmakereport when set, a red test files a fault report TAFHEADLESS conftest.py, BaseTest 1 for CI; headed is the default TAFSLOWMO conftest.py, BaseTest ms between Playwright actions TAFLIVE tests/testlive.py opts the live acceptance tests in TAFBASEURL appears in taf/emitpython.py, taf/emitjava.py, and every emitted conftest.py/BaseTest.java. TAFIKC3WS drives CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink. Touch it bash env grep TAF what is set right now — usually nothing TAFBASEURL=http://x .venv/bin/python -m taf check suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json Neighbors The virtual environment (above); the result sink the TAFIKC3WS variable enables; CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path."},{"t":"The artifacts","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts.html","s":"draft","x":"The artifacts Definition Everything a run leaves behind, under a stable layout — results/ / / / carrying the video, the .srt subtitle sidecar, the DOM and screenshot on failure, the log slice. The old framework's CiTestsRunner established the contract: dated folders, suiteName.txt/publicURL.txt sidecars the report generator consumed. Why it exists Evidence outlives the run. A suite without artifacts answers only \"did it pass\"; with them it answers \"what happened\" — the difference between a test framework and a test observatory. The layout being declared and stable is what lets separate tooling (the HTML matrix report, a fault report, a human browsing) read it without asking the runner anything. In this project Today: the IKC3 sink ships DOM+URL into a fault report (CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink); pytest's own .pytestcache holds the rerun ledger. The results/ / layout, video, and the golden-file naming grammar are lanes J-10 and L-12. Touch it bash ls generated/python/ikc3-tracker/.pytestcache/v/cache/lastfailed 2 /dev/null Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file, CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track, CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun."},{"t":"The preflight","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-preflight","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-preflight.html","s":"draft","x":"The preflight Definition A declared gate before a run — requires: in the model (mail-server up, appium listening) — checked once, failing the whole suite fast and saying why. Its runtime form is the circuit breaker: the old framework's suiteStopReason flag that, once set (the mail server died), failed every subsequent test immediately rather than logging 40 identical red herrings. Why it exists Forty tests all failing \"connection refused\" is noise that costs an hour of triage before anyone asks whether the server was even up. Refusing early — with a named reason — is the same honesty the model loader applies statically, applied to a run's prerequisites. A suite that knows its requirements stops spending test failures on infrastructure facts. In this project Planned — lane L-12. The static analogue exists: taf check's refusals list every reason instead of dying on the first; requires: would be the run-time version of the same contract. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m taf check suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json refusals — the static form of the same idea Neighbors CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink, CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun — a preflight failure shouldn't file 40 reports, it should file one."},{"t":"The subtitle track","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track.html","s":"draft","x":"The subtitle track Definition An .srt sidecar beside the run video: each narrated step (CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label) mapped onto video timecodes — 1 / 00:00:04,200 -- 00:00:05,000 / Clicking 'Sign In' on 'Login'. The run replays readable — a watcher sees what the suite was doing, not just where the mouse was. Why it exists The old framework wired all five teardown paths to capture a video- stop timestamp and call createSubTitlesFile — a writer that was only ever committed commented out. The intent was fully built except for the last function: narration existed (canonicalName), timestamps existed (setStartVideo), the video existed — only the SRT serializer was missing. In generated code it's nearly free: emitted steps know their text and their clock, so a teardown writes the file. In this project Planned — the narration half is lane H-8 (label), the video half is lane J-10's artifact layout (CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts); the .srt writer is the cheap tail of both. Touch it Not yet — the prerequisites it needs are the labels and the video. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-006-01-the-label, CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell (a dwell is where a caption breathes), CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts."},{"t":"The three levels","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01-the-three-levels","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01-the-three-levels.html","s":"draft","x":"The three levels Definition tafbase is three activities, not one — and the reader is always only one of them at a time: Build — the framework itself: the loader, the emitters, the parity contract. Artifact: taf/, tests/. Model — applying the DSL to one SUT: declaring its states, elements, actions, journeys. Artifact: suts/.sut.model.json. Use — the generated suite running under the framework. Artifact: generated/. Why it exists The DSL First promise is a level boundary: the model author benefits without knowing the machinery — no metaprogramming, no emitters, no compiler mechanics. The promise is kept by keeping the levels apart: suts/ is data to level 1, authored input to level 2, and invisible to level 3 (who only ever sees generated code). In this project The repo layout is the separation: taf/ is Build, suts/ is Model, generated/ is Use. SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 mirrors it — Parts 1–2 are the model author's surface, Part 3 the framework's contract. CONTRIBUTING.md is the Build level's handbook; suts/ and generated/ each carry their own doc. Touch it bash python -m taf check suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json Model level .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests -q Build level .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q Use level Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model (level 2's subject), CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path (which personas take which path)."},{"t":"Arrange–Act–Assert","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert.html","s":"draft","x":"Arrange–Act–Assert Definition The shape of a readable test: arrange the preconditions, act once, assert the outcome. Three phases, in that order, visible in the code. python def testticketmovestotriage(boardpage): ticket = boardpage.newticket(\"the report\") arrange ticket.moveto(\"triage\") act expect(boardpage.ticket(\"the report\")).tobein(\"triage\") assert Why it exists A test is documentation that runs. When the three phases are separated, a reader sees what is tested before how; when they interleave, the intent drowns in mechanics. In tafbase the phases come from the model: a journey's setup is its start state, the act is its steps, and the assertions are the state markers and expect-final the emitter writes for every arrival — so generated tests are AAA by construction. In this repo Generated suites under tools/ (lane B-2). Emitted tests carry @concept CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert."},{"t":"The failure funnel","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel.html","s":"draft","x":"The failure funnel Definition One place every failure passes through — capture what the screen was (DOM, screenshot, URL, which step), then fail. The old framework called it teststepFail: every step def, every wait, funneled into the same routine that grabbed the DOM, took the driver-appropriate screenshot, and failed once. Why it exists Scattered failure handling loses evidence — each try invents its own half-broken capture. One funnel means one place to attach capture, reporting, and debugging; a red test leaves evidence, not just a stack. The companion idea — dead-session recovery — came from the same scar: a driver that died mid-wait was re-acquired rather than destroying the whole run. In this project pytestruntestmakereport in the generated conftest.py is the funnel: a red call → URL + DOM captured (guarded against mid-navigation), → ikc3sink.filereport → a tracker fault report. The capture half (screenshot, console slice) is lane J-10/L-12; the recover half is deferred (short tests rarely need it). Touch it bash TAFIKC3WS=ws://127.0.0.1:4856/bus-ws TAFBASEURL=http://127.0.0.1:9 .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q a red test files a report — printed as 'fault report filed: ' Neighbors CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink, CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts, CONCEPT-testing-v1-005-01-the-debug-run."},{"t":"The golden file","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file.html","s":"draft","x":"The golden file Definition An expected artifact — a baseline screenshot — that a compare screen step diffs the actual against, plus the discipline around it: a filename grammar (TCIDrunresstep{Expected,Actual,Diff}.PNG), tolerance and excluded regions per device, and human approval: a person promotes Actual to Expected via a selection file, never by auto-rebaselining. Why it exists A picture asserts what fifty locators cannot. The old framework made screenshots the golden standard, with ALT baselines for legitimate variants, exclusion masks for the iPhone notch, and a scanner that rendered a report grouping diffs. The approval loop is the non-obvious gem: goldens go stale because the SUT legitimately changed — a person must bless the new truth, or the suite silently re-baselines its own drift. In this project Planned — lane J-10. The CONCEPT-dsl-v1-009-01-the-test-id and CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-the-artifacts layout land first, because the filename grammar needs both. Touch it Not yet — the nearest live analogue is the marker assertion in every state (expectarrived). Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-008-01-the-image-step, CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-subtitle-track (the step's caption is the same step's name)."},{"t":"The rerun","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-004-01-the-rerun.html","s":"draft","x":"The rerun Definition A manifest of what failed, written at run end; the next run replays only those. Cucumber's rerun plugin wrote target/rerun.txt and a dedicated runner consumed it — red suite, fix, rerun the reds only. Why it exists A suite of any size makes \"run everything again\" the expensive answer to \"did the fix work\". The rerun manifest closes the fix→verify loop in minutes instead of re-running the world. It also records what did fail — the ledger a repair proves against (the full E-5 criterion: fail → report → fix → repaired). In this project Python already has it for free — pytest's --lf/--last-failed reads its own cache. Lane L-12 makes it a framework feature: a generated rerun manifest keyed on CONCEPT-dsl-v1-009-01-the-test-id so Java gets the same replay and a repair walk can rerun exactly the journey that failed. Touch it bash .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker --lf -q Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel, CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-preflight."},{"t":"The debug run","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-005-01-the-debug-run","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-005-01-the-debug-run.html","s":"draft","x":"The debug run Definition A mode where a failing test stays: breakpoint() / PWDEBUG lands at the failing step, the browser is not torn down, and the runner lets you poke the live page. The old framework had breakPoint() (an empty method to hang an IDE breakpoint on), testIsUnderTest and keepBrowserOpen — all the affordances for interrogating a failure instead of reading it. Why it exists A failure you can inspect beats a failure you read. Most flaky-bug hunting is not \"does it fail\" but \"what did it look like the moment it did\" — and that question is only answerable if the run doesn't close the window on the way out. In this project Lane L-12 — --debug keeps the browser open and breaks on failure. Today: PWDEBUG=1 opens Playwright's inspector, TAFHEADLESS unset + TAFSLOWMO makes the run watchable, and CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell holds a step long enough to see. Touch it bash PWDEBUG=1 .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel, CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-environment."},{"t":"The living spec","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-006-01-the-living-spec","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-006-01-the-living-spec.html","s":"draft","x":"The living spec Definition A specification whose examples execute — the old framework's Concordion fixtures returned display strings from action methods so the rendered spec embedded the actual outcome beside the expected one; its StoryboardExtension drew a per-step screenshot strip inside the spec document. Why it exists A spec that runs cannot drift silently — the document is the test, so an outdated claim is a red test. It's the same instinct as our release-note-first discipline (write the note before the work) taken one step further: the document doesn't just precede the work, it proves it on every run. In this project Deferred — the nearest live practice is the release-note-as-acceptance in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 and taf.manifest.json as traceability. A living-spec emitter would render a document whose expected/actual cells fill at run time; it belongs with J-10's report work. Touch it Read the manifest: generated/python/ikc3-tracker/taf.manifest.json is the machine-readable ancestor of the idea — declared vs run. Neighbors CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert, CONCEPT-testing-v1-003-01-the-golden-file."},{"t":"The mail oracle","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-007-01-the-mail-oracle","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-007-01-the-mail-oracle.html","s":"draft","x":"The mail oracle Definition Verifying email: snapshot the inbox before the action, then poll only messages that arrived after a marked timestamp, comparing the body against a template whose volatile fields are masked (XPLACEHOLDERX, regex-masked Date:/Deposit ID:). Why it exists Mail is the canonical asynchronous oracle — you can't wait for it, you can only look for what wasn't there before and verify it loosely enough that volatile fields don't flake it. The old framework's tricks were the reusable pearls: the baseline snapshot (only new messages count), the sparse poll schedule ({2,15,25,43,...,179}s over a 182s budget — early checks are cheap, patience grows), and the masked template compare. It even scraped an MFA code back out of the mail HTML and typed it into the page — closing a full verify-by-mail loop. In this project Deferred — needs a mail-capable SUT. The pattern generalizes: any async oracle (a webhook, a queued job) wants snapshot-then-poll-new. Touch it Not implemented — RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01, item 18. Neighbors CONCEPT-dsl-v1-010-01-the-fixture (arrange via API), CONCEPT-testing-v1-002-01-the-failure-funnel."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone","i":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone Decided: 2026-10-14 · George Petrov + Devin Decisions 1. tafbase is its own repository and its own corpus. It does not live inside IKC3. gaks.project.json declares it; the static GAKS bundle of PLAN-ikc-v3-079-01 delivers the corpus tools. This is the first real consumer of the foreign-project path. 2. The DSL is JSON, not EDN. The SUT model must be readable and writable from Java and Python with no Clojure dependency — the same reason gaks.project.json replaced the EDN declaration in IKC3. 3. Generate, don't interpret. Emitters write committed, reviewable test files (testlogin.py, LoginJourneyTest.java). A generic runtime interpreter was considered and refused: generated code diffs in a PR are legible; hidden magic is not. 4. Tracker integrations are declared adapters, not a hard dependency. tafbase core runs with no IKC3. The IKC3 adapter (fault reports, repaired-on-merge) is the first sink and the demo loop; JIRA and GitHub Issues adapters are additions of the same kind. 5. The IKC3 tracker is the first SUT, not a component of tafbase. Dogfooding means tests exercise the real platform and file reports into it — not that tafbase embeds it. 6. MIT license. The goals are exposure and adoption; copyleft and dual-licensing are refused. Consequences Doc ids take the taf subproject prefix (PLAN-taf-v1-NNN-NN); numbering is this repository's own. The IKC3 deployment tracker can file tickets for tafbase work only if this corpus is registered as a vault of the deployment — an open question, deferred to the first lane."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01 — the delivered project is a learning surface","i":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01 — the delivered project is a learning surface Decided: 2026-10-14 · George Petrov + Devin Context The precedent is Python-Selenium-Chrome-Demo/v.0.1/docs/concepts.md: a hand-written glossary of 60 concepts — Python, Selenium, pytest, design patterns, testing — each with a definition, an example, and a \"Used in\" pointer. It was good teaching material and it could rot: the pointers were prose, checked by nobody. Decisions 7. The corpus teaches. CONCEPT documents in arch-root/concepts/ cover the implicit and explicit concepts the project embodies, across four domains: the language and runner (Python: fixtures, yield, dataclasses; pytest/JUnit: discovery, markers, parametrize), the tool (Playwright/Selenium: locators, auto-waiting, browser contexts), test design (AAA, page object, isolation, flaky tests), and the DSL itself (model, emitter, manifest, parity, refusal). A concept is a page — a definition, a small example, where it lives in this repo — never a reference manual. 8. Citations are checked, not promised. Generated code carries @concept CONCEPT-taf-v1-NNN-NN- comment lines, the form the GAKS code.annotation-pattern already checks (code-link-unresolved fails when a cited concept has no document). The emitter decides which concepts each generated file demonstrates and writes the annotations; the manifest records the concept ids per test. An annotation with no document is a defect the checker refuses. A document nothing cites is not: a concept may be written before the code that embodies it — the learning path marks such pages as waiting on their lane, and a corpus report may list them, but the check does not fail on them. 9. A guided path orders the glossary. The corpus is flat and wiki-linked, but a learner needs a sequence. One concept document — CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path — is the ordered index: what a test is → AAA → locators → fixtures → the model → the emitters. Junior-first; each entry names its prerequisites. 10. PRINCIPLE documents join the corpus. tafbase's gaks.project.json declares the PRINCIPLE kind so the platform's own rules (DSL First, release-note-first, one-form-only) are corpus documents that code and plans can cite, not folklore in a README. Consequences Every emitter lane ships the concept documents its generated code cites; the corpus check is the gate — a lane that emits an uncited concept does not pass. The learning surface is a property of the delivered bundle, not of ikc3: gaks/site/ renders the concepts and their backlinks with no server, on tafbase.com or off a disk."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-003-01 — three audiences, and who decides what","i":"ADR-taf-v1-003-01-the-audiences","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-003-01-the-audiences.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-003-01 — three audiences, and who decides what Decided: 2026-10-16 · George Petrov + Devin Context The harvest debate surfaced a recurring confusion: a fork between Playwright's native screenshot compare and a hand-rolled filename grammar was put to a stakeholder whose interest is what a run produces, not how the diff engine works. Asking it conflated roles — the same mixing CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01 names for artifacts must hold for decisions too. Decisions Three audiences, and a decision belongs to whoever's surface it changes: Framework creator — owns internals: how the generator works, which library diffs pixels, the journal's format, schema mechanics. Surface: taf/, tests/, this ADR log, CONTRIBUTING.md. Rulings are made here, by whoever writes the code, and recorded as ADRs — reviewable after the fact, not adjudicated before it. Test creator (model author) — owns the vocabulary: which clauses a model may declare. Surface: suts/, SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 Parts 1–2, the dsl concepts. A decision only reaches this audience when it adds, removes, or renames something the model can say. Results consumer — owns what a run shows: the report, the artifacts, the narration. Surface: generated/ output, manifests, video + .srt, fault reports. A decision reaches this audience when it changes what a run leaves behind. Capability vs selection. The artifact surface is two-layered: the framework creator makes an artifact possible (the diff engine, the recorder, the .srt writer — ADR territory), and the framework user selects and configures it — what is collected, where it lands, which sink reports go to. Mechanism vs policy: capability is built once and recorded in an ADR; selection is declared, per run via the TAF environment or per suite via the model — whichever owns the variation. The rule: put no internal fork to a creator or consumer. Put no vocabulary or visibility question to a creator without naming the surface it changes. An ADR is the record of a creator decision; a spec clause is the record of a vocabulary one; a release note is the record of a visible one. Consequences The evidence-stack fork is settled by its owner, not escalated: Playwright's native compare (tohavescreenshot/hasScreenshot) under tafbase's own artifact layout — we do not rebuild a diff engine to keep a filename. A per-test taf.journal.jsonl is the shared timestamped substrate — .srt, rerun manifests, and fault reports read it; it is designed once. The journey name is the test id; test-id is a naming rule, not a new field. targets: becomes a declared model section — a vocabulary change, so it is a spec clause, not an emitter detail."},{"t":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework","i":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase","h":"doc/plans/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework Status: in progress tafbase declares a system under test (SUT) once, as data, and generates real test suites from the declaration. Python+Playwright is the first emitter; Java is the second, and the second exists to make the parity claim true. Generated suites report into pluggable tracker adapters; the first adapter is IKC3's fault reports, so a red test files a bug into the very platform the tests exercise. Release note (draft) New: a SUT declared once in JSON runs as a Python+Playwright suite; the same declaration refuses the same broken model the same way. Accepted by: T-taf-001 (parity of refusal, env -i, python3 only) New: a failed generated test files a fault report into a configured tracker through a declared adapter; the IKC3 adapter is the first. Accepted by: T-taf-002 Internal: the Java+Playwright emitter proves the same declaration generates equivalent suites in two languages. Accepted by: T-taf-003 (Python and Java emitters agree on the suite) What this does not do v1 generates static journeys only. Model-based generation — walks of the SUT graph, coverage-directed paths, journal replay — is declared in ADR-taf-v1-001-01 and arrives by lanes, not all at once. Lanes ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1 — the SUT model is JSON The contract: SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase. A SUT declares states, transitions and journeys in one JSON document. The loader validates shape, refusals name the broken clause, and no ambiguous form is guessed — one form only. Release note (draft) New: sut.model.json declares pages, actions and journeys; a broken model names its fault. Accepted by: T-taf-001 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/B-2 — the Python+Playwright emitter From a valid model, taf emit --lang python writes a runnable Playwright+pytest suite: page objects, journey tests, and a manifest mapping each test back to the model element that produced it. Release note (draft) New: one declaration produces a committed, reviewable pytest suite. Accepted by: T-taf-004 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/C-3 — tracker adapters and fault reports Progress (2026-10-15). The IKC3 sink exists and works live: taf/sinkikc3.py dispatches :support/file-report over /bus-ws (EDN envelope, ikc3-bus subprotocol, no token needed where authz is off). Emitted verbatim into each Python suite as ikc3sink.py; the generated pytestruntestmakereport hook files on any red test when TAFIKC3WS is set, carrying page URL + DOM. Proven against a probe bus: a suite pointed at a dead SUT filed one report per red test (tests/testlive.py, opt-in). Still open: the Java-side hook, screenshot/attachment upload, auth-token support, a second (JIRA-shaped) adapter. A test outcome flows to a declared sink. The IKC3 adapter files a fault report and links it to the test's model element; adapters are a declared kind so JIRA or GitHub Issues are additions, not rewrites. Release note (draft) New: a red test files a fault report into IKC3, named for i"},{"t":"The interview surface","i":"RESEARCH-taf-v1-001-01-the-interview-surface","h":"doc/research/RESEARCH-taf-v1-001-01-the-interview-surface.html","s":"draft","x":"The interview surface What this is A coverage map: the corpus's concepts against the questions the market actually asks. Source: a widely-shared \"Top 25 Playwright interview questions\" list (LinkedIn, 2026 SDET interview prep). The corpus's stated job is to serve as a learning surface and interview prep — this document measures that promise against an external yardstick and names the gaps as work. The map interview asks covered by state --- --- --- --- 1 Playwright vs Selenium — gap 2 Project setup (TypeScript assumed) — gap 3 Browser / BrowserContext / Page CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context part 4 Auto-waiting, actionability CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting covered 5 locator() vs page.$() CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder part — locator-vs-$ deserves a line 6 Dynamic elements, flaky tests — gap 7 getByRole vs getByText vs css/xpath CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder covered 8 Dropdowns, checkboxes, alerts, iframes — gap 9 File upload/download — gap 10 Screenshots/videos on failure CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink (our answer is richer: DOM + report) part 11 API testing via request context the model's api/save/expect steps covered 12 Authentication reuse — gap 13 storageState — gap 14 Parallel execution, workers — gap 15 Sharding — gap 16 Retries, flaky-test investigation — gap 17 beforeEach/afterEach CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-the-junit-lifecycle, CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture covered (in two idioms) 18 Framework architecture: POM + fixtures CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object + the whole generated layout covered — our strongest answer 19 HTML reports the manifest + sink part — ours is better (traceable), say so 20 CI/CD integration — gap 21 Cross-browser (chromium/firefox/webkit) — gap — model has no browser clause yet 22 API mocking, network interception — gap 23 Debugging: trace viewer, headed, inspector — gap — not asked but we answer it seeded walks, parity contracts, checked citations, refusals our differentiators What the map says Seven covered outright, 4 partial, 14 gaps. The gaps cluster into themes, each of which is a lane, not just a page: Flakiness & resilience (6, 16): retry policy, quarantine — a real feature lane, not only docs. Element dynamics (8, 9): dropdowns/iframes/uploads — the step language grows (select exists; frame, upload, dialog don't). Auth & state reuse (12, 13): storageState maps cleanly to a model clause — declared once, applied to journeys that need it. Execution control (14, 15, 20, 21): workers, shards, CI matrix, browsers — belongs in a run clause or per-suite config. Observability (10, 19, 22, 23): screenshots on failure, Playwright trace, API mocking — lanes of their own. The TypeScript question The market's assumed Playwright language is TypeScript (the question list presumes it). A third emitter is the right move — and cheap: taf/ops.py already carries the canonical plan, so emitts.py renders syntax only, and the parity tests generalize t"},{"t":"The harvest from qa-tafbase","i":"RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01-the-harvest-from-qa-tafbase","h":"doc/research/RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01-the-harvest-from-qa-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"The harvest from qa-tafbase What this is A previous-generation framework — a Java/Selenium/Cucumber suite (testsbase engine + per-product modules, JUnit4, Appium) — was mapped end-to-end for reusable design. This document keeps the result and the decisions it produced. The raw harvest — 27 ideas with evidence paths, translations and costs — is RESEARCH-taf-v1-003-01-the-harvest-in-detail. The rules the harvest confirmed Declared beats interpreted. What the old runtime computed — device capability strings, tag→env mapping, layered properties — tafbase emits at codegen. The generator absorbs what a factory once computed. Dwell is for observation, not synchronization. visibilitySleep (a global + per-call-site Thread.sleep around every action) answered two needs at once: letting JS settle and letting a human watch. Auto-waiting markers own the first; a declared dwell owns the second. They must not fuse — sleeps-for-correctness is the disease this framework is built to avoid. Adopted (lanes in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01) idea becomes lane --- --- --- visibilitySleep pacing dwell — per-step/ms or a suite profile, emitted as a literal H-8 named wait vocabulary + soft/hard wait/check step forms, wait any (X Y) H-8 version × language locator matrix versioned elements, generated lookup tables I-9 screenshot grammar + approval loop compare screen step, TCIDrunresstep{E,A,D}, taf promote J-10 capability token (Pixel9ProXLchrome) device:/env: model header emitting the driver block K-11 column-model schema (its TSV interpreter) stays as proof the grammar works — the tafbase DSL is its compile-time heir (settled) rerun.txt failed-only replay taf run --last-failed manifest L-12 debug affordances --debug: keep browser open, break on failure L-12 circuit breaker / preflight requires: model clause → emitted preflight L-12 self-narrating actions required human label on elements/steps → emitted logs H-8 the never-built .srt subtitles narrated steps + timestamps → sidecar .srt beside run video H-8 (with J-10 artifacts) The .srt item deserves a note: all five teardown paths of the old framework captured a video-stop timestamp and called a createSubTitlesFile that was only ever committed commented out — the intent was a subtitle track mapping each narrated action line (Clicking 'X' on 'Y') onto video timecodes. The hard half — timestamped narration — already existed. In generated code both halves are cheap: emitted steps know their text and their clock, so a teardown writer emits results/ / .srt beside the video. A run becomes replayable and readable. Deliberately left behind PPTX reports, Robot/AWT screen capture, hotkey-driven video, Swing overlay strips, VM-awake mouse jiggles, X:-drive archiving, hardcoded notification tokens. Playwright viewport capture + HTML reports + committed artifacts replace the whole cluster."},{"t":"The harvest in detail — evidence appendix","i":"RESEARCH-taf-v1-003-01-the-harvest-in-detail","h":"doc/research/RESEARCH-taf-v1-003-01-the-harvest-in-detail.html","s":"draft","x":"The harvest in detail — evidence appendix The full evidence behind RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01: every idea recovered from the previous-generation framework (a Maven multi-module Java/Selenium/Cucumber/Appium suite — testsbase engine plus per-product modules), with the file that proves it, the translation to generated code, and where it landed in this corpus. Architecture context: JUnit4 + Cucumber 7.13 + Selenium 4.35 + Appium. A prior generation exists underneath (Concordion + a TSV interpreter — item 14). → marks the tafbase landing: a CONCEPT page, a lane of PLAN-taf-v1-001-01, or done. Per-step pacing / dwell 1. visibilitySleep / visiSleep — the global dwell Evidence: testsbase/.../utils/TimeUtils.java:478-498; config visibility.sleep.time=222 (config.properties:32); mutable static Configuration.VISIBILITYSLEEP (utils/Configuration.java:60,192). Called before and after nearly every action (com/fxcm/qa/TestBase.java:809-826). What it is: a Thread.sleep wrapper with two units and a zero-arg overload using the global. Two purposes: let JS/animations settle, and make live-watching pleasant (paired with the Overlay). Overridden per call-site (visiSleep(345) at TestBase.java:1082), per run (config), at runtime (non-final static; even inside JS — setTimeout(()= arguments[0].click(), VISIBILITYSLEEP) at TestBase.java:756,789). Translation: a DSL-level annotation, not a sleep — resolved at codegen into per-step literals. Cost: small. → done — {\"dwell\": }; CONCEPT-dsl-v1-003-01-the-dwell. Browser/driver factory 2. \"Resolution\" — one token drives local emulation AND BrowserStack Evidence: CucumberTestBase.initTest dispatches on \"desktop, mobile, tablet\".contains(resolution) (:139-143); local mobile/tablet via CDP Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride (:550-572); remote via token parsing — w10chrome, iPhone16Prosafari, GalaxyS25Ultrasamsung → os/osVersion/ deviceName/browserName switch (:319-430). Env creds override browserstack.yml (:289-290); buildIdentifier=TESTRUNID, sessionName=testNameexample (:306-307). Translation: already a mini-DSL — device: Pixel9ProXLchrome emits the exact capabilities object; zero runtime parsing, compile-time vocabulary. Cost: medium. → lane K-11; CONCEPT-dsl-v1-012-01-the-target. 3. Tag → environment → URL AND locator-version selection Evidence: isUatTest maps @qaenv/@uatenv/@browserstack to suiteName + testUrl + DEFAULTVERSION (CucumberTestBase.java:128-134,218-239; DEFAULTVERSIONQA/UAT in Configuration.java:28-29,139-148). Translation: env: bakes baseUrl + locator version into the emitted suite. Cost: small. → lane K-11; same concept as item 2. Waits 4. Named wait vocabulary, soft and hard Evidence: waitUntilVisibilityOfElement, waitLongVisibilityOfElement, waitInvisibilityOfElement, waitUntilClickable, waitUntilVisibilityOfEitherElements, waitUntilVisibilityOfText (TestBase.java:962-1146). Soft variant logs + saves DOM + screenshot, continues (waitUntilVisibilityOfElementParametrized, :985-1011); hard variant fails through test"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model.html","s":"superseded","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model Superseded by SPEC-taf-v1-001-02-the-sut-model — same contract, rewritten normative with numbered clauses; kept as history. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone The design input of lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1. This document is the contract: a loader and an emitter are correct together when each refusal and each generated behavior below holds. Purpose A system under test (SUT) is declared once, as one JSON document. The declaration is the model. From it, emitters write runnable suites (Python+Playwright, later Java+Playwright) that follow Playwright's own rules: semantic locators, auto-waiting assertions, one journey per test. The same document is the graph the MBT lanes walk later — states and transitions are first-class from v1, so no model is written twice. The document One file per SUT, sut.model.json by convention. Top level: json { \"sut\": {\"name\": \"ikc3-tracker\", \"base-url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:4855\"}, \"states\": {}, \"elements\": {}, \"actions\": {}, \"journeys\": {}, \"endpoints\": {} } sut name (string, required) names the SUT in refusals and the manifest. base-url (string, required) is where journeys begin. No environment indirection in v1: a different deployment is a different file, or a later lane adds env. states A state is a place a journey can be — a page, a dialog, a verified condition. A state is recognized, not assumed: json \"states\": { \"board\": { \"route\": \"/index.html?island=board\", \"marker\": {\"role\": \"heading\", \"name\": \"Board\"} } } route (optional) is the path under base-url that reaches the state directly. marker (required) is a locator that a generated test asserts visible after every arrival — a state is entered only when the SUT proves it. That one rule is what makes generated tests self-verifying instead of hopeful. elements Named locators, declared once, reused everywhere: json \"elements\": { \"new-ticket-button\": {\"role\": \"button\", \"name\": \"New ticket\"}, \"title-field\": {\"label\": \"Title\"}, \"report-card\": {\"testid\": \"report-card\"}, \"legacy-panel\": {\"css\": \".panel--legacy\"} } THE LOCATOR LADDER is the \"proper use\" clause, encoded, not documented: 1. role + name — the accessibility query, preferred 2. label / text / placeholder — user-visible queries 3. testid — the deliberate escape (data-testid) 4. css / xpath — the last resort, allowed and flagged fragile in the manifest A locator with no usable form is refused :bad-locator. The ladder is the emitter's only ordering; a model does not say which rung a locator sits on, the shape does. actions An action is a step the SUT offers — the transitions of the graph: json \"actions\": { \"move-to-triage\": { \"from\": \"backlog\", \"to\": \"triage\", \"do\": [ {\"click\": \"new-ticket-button\"}, {\"fill\": {\"element\": \"title-field\", \"value\": \"the report\"}}, {\"click\": \"save-button\"}, {\"api\": \"move-ticket\", \"args\": {\"category\": \"triage\"}} ] } } from and to name states and are optional per action, required for the graph to be a graph: an acti"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-02 — the SUT model","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-02-the-sut-model","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-02-the-sut-model.html","s":"superseded","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-02 — the SUT model Superseded by SPEC-taf-v1-001-03-the-sut-language — the same contract reframed as a language specification: the JSON is the concrete syntax of the DSL, not the subject. Kept as history. Purpose tafbase is a test automation framework: a web application is described once, as one JSON document, and that document is compiled into runnable browser and API test suites — Playwright for Python and Java today. This document specifies that JSON document. It is the contract between a model author and the emitters: the implementation is correct when all clauses in this document hold. Read this document on its own — it uses no other document of this project. Keywords must, must not, should, and may are normative, in the RFC 2119 sense. Clauses carry identifiers (SM-n) so a test, a review, or a refusal can name what it verifies. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface Terms term meaning --- --- SUT the system under test — the application the generated suite exercises model one JSON document declaring a SUT's states, elements, actions, journeys, endpoints state a recognizable place in the SUT — a page, a dialog — proven by its marker marker a locator a generated test asserts visible on arrival at a state locator one form of finding an element — role+name, label, text, placeholder, testid, css, xpath action a named step the SUT offers; with from/to it is an edge of the graph journey a named path through the graph; emitted as one test refusal a named rejection {clause, reason, message}; a broken model emits nothing emitter a code generator: a model in, a runnable suite out manifest taf.manifest.json — emitted beside the tests, mapping each test to its model elements SM-1 · One file, JSON A SUT is declared in one file, .sut.model.json by convention. It must be a JSON object; a document that is not is refused :not-json. A name declared twice in one clause is refused :duplicate-name — JSON's silent last-write-wins is not accepted. Top level: json { \"sut\": {\"name\": \"ikc3-tracker\", \"base-url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:4856\"}, \"states\": {}, \"elements\": {}, \"actions\": {}, \"journeys\": {}, \"endpoints\": {} } sut.name and sut.base-url are required (:missing-field). A model with no states and no journeys is refused :empty-model. elements, actions, and endpoints may be absent or empty. SM-2 · States are recognized, not assumed json \"states\": { \"board\": {\"route\": \"/index.html?island=board\", \"marker\": {\"role\": \"heading\", \"name\": \"Board\"}} } A state must declare marker, a locator (SM-3); a generated test must assert it visible on every arrival — the SUT proves the state, the test does not assume it (:missing-field without one). route is optional; when present it is the path under base-url that reaches the state directly. A state no journey can reach is refused :unreachable — a declared state with no path to it is a dead declaration. SM-3 · The locator ladder A locator must take "},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-03 — the SUT language","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-03-the-sut-language","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-03-the-sut-language.html","s":"superseded","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-03 — the SUT language Superseded by SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase — the subject is the framework, not the language; the DSL is its interface, usable without knowing it is one. Kept as history. Purpose tafbase is a DSL First test automation framework. Its input is a small language for declaring a system under test (SUT): the states a user can reach, the actions that move between them, and the journeys a suite must prove. From one declaration, emitters compile runnable browser and API test suites — Playwright for Python and Java today. This document specifies that language. Part 1 gives the language's constructs and their meaning — the abstract syntax. Part 2 gives the JSON form a model author writes — the concrete syntax. Part 3 gives the contract every implementation must honor: loading, refusals, emission, parity. This document stands alone. Keywords must, must not, should, and may are normative in the RFC 2119 sense. Clauses carry identifiers (SL-n) so a test, a review, or a refusal names what it verifies. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface Terms term meaning --- --- DSL domain-specific language — here, the language this document defines SUT the system under test — the application a generated suite exercises model a program in the SUT language: one declaration of a SUT state a recognizable place in the SUT, proven by its marker marker a locator a generated test asserts visible on arrival locator one way to find an element — role+name, label, text, placeholder, testid, css, xpath action a named step the SUT offers; with from/to it is an edge journey a named path through the graph; compiled to one test refusal a named rejection {clause, reason, message}; a rejected model emits nothing emitter a compiler backend: a model in, a runnable suite out manifest taf.manifest.json — maps each emitted test to its model elements Part 1 — The constructs SL-1 · States are recognized, not assumed A state is where a journey can be. It declares a marker — the proof — and may declare a route, the address that reaches it directly. A test arrives at a state only when the SUT shows the marker. This is the language's core semantics: a state is proven, never presumed. SL-2 · Locators form a ladder A locator takes exactly one form, and the forms are ordered by what a user perceives: rung form standing --- --- --- 1 role + name how a user names a control — preferred 2 label / text / placeholder what a user reads 3 testid a deliberate testing hook 4 css / xpath structure, fragile — flagged in the manifest The ladder is a language rule, not a style guide: the shape of a locator chooses its rung, and rung 4 carries a fragile mark into the manifest. SL-3 · Actions are edges An action is a step the SUT offers. An action that declares from and to is an edge of the SUT's graph — the same graph the MBT lanes walk (taf walk), so a model is written once and serves journeys, walks, and cover"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 — tafbase","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 — tafbase Purpose tafbase is a test automation framework. You describe a system under test (SUT) once — its screens, its controls, the journeys that matter — and tafbase generates runnable browser and API test suites: pytest + Playwright for Python, JUnit 5 + Playwright for Java. The description is a small DSL — but using tafbase needs no knowledge of languages or code generation. You fill in a JSON form; the framework turns it into tests you can read, run, and review. Everything in this document is about what you declare and what you get. This document stands alone. Keywords must, must not, should, and may are normative in the RFC 2119 sense. Clauses carry identifiers (TAF-n) so a test, a review, or a refusal names what it verifies. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface Terms term meaning --- --- SUT the system under test — the application a generated suite exercises model your declaration of a SUT, written in tafbase's DSL state a recognizable place in the SUT, proven by its marker marker a locator a generated test asserts visible on arrival locator one way to find an element — role+name, label, text, placeholder, testid, css, xpath action a named step the SUT offers; with from/to it is an edge journey a named path through the SUT; compiled to one test refusal a named rejection {clause, reason, message}; a rejected model emits nothing emitter a generator: a model in, a runnable suite out manifest taf.manifest.json — maps each generated test to your model's elements walk a journey the tool derives by traversing the graph, replayable by seed Part 1 — What you declare TAF-1 · States are recognized, not assumed A state is where a journey can be — a page, a dialog, a verified condition. It declares a marker: the visible proof. A test arrives at a state only when the SUT shows that proof. A state may also declare a route, the address that reaches it directly. TAF-2 · Locators form a ladder A locator takes exactly one form. The forms are ordered by what a user perceives, and the order is a rule of the framework, not a style guide: rung form standing --- --- --- 1 role + name how a user names a control — preferred 2 label / text / placeholder what a user reads 3 testid a deliberate testing hook 4 css / xpath structure, fragile — flagged in the manifest The shape of a locator chooses its rung; you never declare one. Named locators are declared once in elements and used by name everywhere. TAF-3 · Actions are edges An action is a step the SUT offers. An action with from and to is an edge of the SUT's graph — which is also what taf walk traverses, so a model serves journeys, walks, and coverage without being written twice. An action's do list composes steps: act (click, fill, check, select), move (navigate), observe (wait-for, wait-any, expect — soft: true warns and continues), call the API (api, save), pace (dwell — a pause for a watching human, never a synchro"}];
